-- dispatch: a PRIVATE `eq` wins for this module's own uses and does NOT
-- suppress the derived row every other module needs (§3.3 step 1, §8.5).
--
-- Two answers to one name, and the golden is both of them at once.
--
-- `sameMajor`'s `==` is a use INSIDE the declaring module, where §3.3 step
-- 1 says "the declaring module's own value `name`, `pub` (or any, in the
-- same module)" — so the site is `top eq` and the private declaration wins.
--
-- The `derived 0 eq` row beside it is what a DEPENDENT sees. `Interface`
-- maps only the `pub` entries, so a module that writes `Id 3 4 == Id 3 99`
-- cannot reach this `eq` at all: it derives, and names `Id$$eq` in THIS
-- module by §8.5. The eager pass therefore has to write that function.
-- Excluding the row on any declaration of the name — `pub` or not — made
-- the dependent import a name no module exported: exit 0, and a
-- `SyntaxError` at load.
pub type Id
    = Id Int Int


eq : Id, Id -> Bool
eq a b =
    case a of
        Id majorA _ ->
            case b of
                Id majorB _ ->
                    majorA == majorB


pub sameMajor : Id, Id -> Bool
sameMajor a b =
    a == b
